---
// Lesson 2.3a — Health Potions & the Max-Health Cap
// The first real if statement: cap healing at max health. Selection!
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>2.3a — Health Potions &amp; the Max-Health Cap</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    You can take damage; now undo it. But healing without a cap breaks
    the game — the potion becomes god mode. Today: your first real
    <strong>if</strong> statement, protecting a rule the game designer
    cares about.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Predict First</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>The starter has coins (trigger, +1) and spikes (collision, −1).
    It has no potion. Predict the two bugs a naive potion causes
    before you write anything:</p>
    <ol>
        <li>You have 3 health, drink 5 potions. What number is health?
            Should it be? What does the Console say vs what the game
            <em>should</em> say?</li>
        <li>At what health value does drinking a potion stop matter
            — and should it still "work"? (A designer question: is
            a wasted potion annoying or fair?)</li>
    </ol>

    <p>
        The AP exam calls this <strong>selection</strong> — an if that
        changes behavior based on a condition. You have technically
        seen ifs already (the tag checks); this is the first one that
        encodes a <em>rule</em> instead of an identity. Same keyword,
        different job.
    </p>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — the potion object.</strong> Make a Potion from
    the pickup pattern: a GameObject with a SpriteRenderer (any
    distinctive sprite), a BoxCollider2D with <strong>Is Trigger
    checked</strong>, and the tag <strong>Potion</strong> (add it in
    the tag list). Drag it to Assets/Prefabs to make it a prefab, like
    the Coin.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — the naive version.</strong> In
    PlayerController.OnTriggerEnter2D, add a branch:</p>
    <pre><code>if (other.CompareTag("Potion"))
{
    if (StatManager.manager != null)
    {
        StatManager.manager.ChangeHealth(1);
    }
    Destroy(other.gameObject);
}</code></pre>

    <p>Run it, heal to full, keep drinking. Watch the Console. Health
    is now 6, 7, 8 — the game's rule is gone. You built the exploit
    yourself, on purpose. Fixing it comes next.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — the cap.</strong> Two ways to say "never more
    than 3":</p>
    <pre><code>// Option A: guard inside StatManager.ChangeHealth
public void ChangeHealth(int amount)
{
    health += amount;
    if (health &gt; 3)
    {
        health = 3;
    }
    Debug.Log("Health Changed! New Health: " + health);
    ...
}

// Option B: the Math class
health = Mathf.Min(health, 3);</code></pre>

    <p>Read both. Which object should own the cap — the potion (it
    heals), the player (it gets healed), or the StatManager (it owns
    health)? The starter's answer is the StatManager, because the rule
    protects <em>health</em>, and health is the StatManager's job.
    Everything that touches health must obey the same cap — that is
    the whole argument for putting the rule in one place.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Overheal and friends</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>What happens if the cap is &gt; 3 but health starts at
                3 and you drink at exactly full? Trace it line by line.</li>
            <li>Change the cap to &lt; 1 in the wrong direction — say
                <code>if (health &lt; 1) { health = 1; }</code>. Now
                spike yourself to zero. What rule did you just invent?</li>
            <li>Put the cap in the potion branch instead of ChangeHealth.
                Why is that the same today but fragile the moment a
                second healing item appears?</li>
            <li>What does Mathf.Min(health, 3) return when health is
                −2? Is the min cap enough for the whole system — or do
                you also need the death check to catch it?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Design the economy</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Should a potion be worth 1 health or 2? Make potions
                spawn rarely and be worth 2 — what breaks, and what
                does "rare" mean in a level design sense?</li>
            <li>Turn the max into a [SerializeField] so you can tune
                maxHealth in the Inspector like xSpeed. Where exactly
                do you declare it — and what replaces every hardcoded
                3 in ChangeHealth?</li>
            <li>Hard: full health + potion = wasted. Make the potion
                NOT be destroyed if it heals nothing. What does the
                player feel, and which line did you touch?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Build a pickup object with trigger + custom tag from scratch</li>
            <li>Explain selection: the if that encodes a rule vs the if that checks identity</li>
            <li>Argue where a rule belongs (StatManager vs the item) and why</li>
            <li>Trace an overheal exploit and cap it two ways</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/index.html">Unit 2: Stats &amp; Pickups</a> — the StatManager, caps, and the pickup family</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/triggers-vs-collisions.html">Triggers vs Collisions</a> — why potions trigger</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="2.4a.html">2.4a — Prefabs, Spawning &amp; Playtesting</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="2.3a" title="2.3a Health Potions &amp; the Max-Health Cap — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
